# Multimeter Measurements

## What I did

* Learned how to use a multimeter using the EEVblog as a reference.
* Measured and verified the resistance values of different resistors.
* Used an Arduino Uno to control an SG90 and measured the DC current it drew under different conditions.
* Measured the terminal voltage of a 4×AA battery pack.
* Compared the measured stall current of the SG90 and MG90S with the quoted values in my notes.

## Servo Motor Setup

The servos were controlled using an Arduino Uno. The signal wire of the servo was connected to **digital pin 9**, while the servo was powered from a separate 4×AA battery pack.

The battery positive terminal was connected to the multimeter, with the multimeter connected **in series** with the servo's power supply so that the current flowing through the servo could be measured.

The battery negative terminal was connected to the servo ground and the Arduino ground, giving the Arduino and servo a common ground.

The multimeter was connected to the **10 A current jack** for the higher-current measurements.

## Battery Pack Setup

The battery pack consisted of four AA batteries connected in series.

For measuring voltage, the multimeter was switched to DC voltage mode and connected **in parallel** across the battery pack:

* Red probe → battery positive
* Black probe → battery negative

The pack measured **6.2 V** with no load.

During the stall test, the multimeter was kept across the battery terminals to measure the voltage while the servo was drawing current. The terminal voltage dropped to approximately **5.9 V**.

## Measurements

| Test                               |    Value | Conditions                          |
| ---------------------------------- | -------: | ----------------------------------- |
| SG90 no-load, sweeping 0–180°      |    25 mA | 10 A jack                           |
| SG90 no-load, stationary           | 12–14 mA | Servo stationary                    |
| SG90 holding                       |    30 mA | Eraser placed on arm                |
| SG90 stall                         |  ~650 mA | Horn held, less than 2 s, 10 A jack |
| MG90S stall                        |  ~600 mA | Same method                         |
| Battery pack, open circuit         |    6.2 V | 4×AA in series                      |
| Battery pack, terminal under stall |    5.9 V | During SG90 stall                   |
| Source resistance                  |  ~0.46 Ω | Derived from 0.3 V ÷ 0.65 A         |

## Observation

The main thing I noticed from the servo tests was that the current is closely related to the **position error** the servo is trying to correct.

The eraser produced a relatively small opposing torque, so the servo could maintain its position with only about **30 mA**. When the horn was physically blocked, the servo could not reach the commanded position, producing a much larger position error. The current increased to approximately **650 mA**.

This also explains why simply saying that current increases with the applied load is incomplete. A load matters because it can cause the servo to develop a position error. A stalled servo has a very large persistent position error, which is why the current is much higher.

## Battery Observation

The battery pack measured **6.2 V** with no load and approximately **5.9 V** while the SG90 was stalled.

The voltage drop was therefore:

$$
6.2 - 5.9 = 0.3\text{ V}
$$

Using the measured stall current of approximately **0.65 A**, the effective source resistance was estimated as:

$$
R = \frac{0.3}{0.65}
$$

$$
R \approx 0.46\ \Omega
$$

## Conclusion

This gave me a practical understanding of how a multimeter can be used to measure resistance, voltage, and current, and why the meter has to be connected differently depending on what is being measured.

The servo measurements also showed a clear difference between normal operation, holding a load, and a true stall. The SG90 drew about **30 mA** while holding the eraser but approximately **650 mA** when its horn was blocked.

The measured SG90 stall current also fell within the **500–700 mA** range noted as its quoted stall current.
